<!-- This is an HTML comment, which does not affect the output.
  -->
<!-- You aren't really using paragraphs, so I've modified your source 
  to use constructs in HTML that match what you are really doing.

  I've used three different sizes of heading:
    H1 for the very top level (and I centered it).
    H3 for the section headings.
    H4 for the subsection headings.

  Almost everything else is a list.  There are two main kinds of list:
    DL starts a definition list, which has a "hanging tag".  I use this 
      just to get the list heading in the right place, but it must always 
      be terminated with /DL.  DT can be used to actually place a hanging 
      tag, followed by DD to place a definition.  I only had to do that 
      once, so don't worry about it.
    UL starts an "unordered", or bullet list.  It has to be terminated 
      with /UL.  Each list item within an unordered list should start with 
      an LI.
    OL starts a numbered list, but there's only one of those in the 
      document, so don't worry about it.  Each list item in a numbered 
      list starts with LI, just like the unordered list.

    To keep all your lists lined up and a consistent look to the page, 
      I've gone ahead and started a DL in each section, even if there 
      are no list headers.  That way all the bullets are at the same 
      indentation in every section.

    Everything has been regularized so things are done the same way over
      and over, so once you've done or seen one, hopefully the new sections 
      can be done the same way.

    Hope this helps!

    Vince
  -->
<!-- The cruft below is supposed to make the lists look the same on
     everyone's browser.  You can ignore it and proceed after the BODY.
  -->
<HEAD>
<STYLE TYPE=text/css>
body {backgroundfoo:#fff;}
#menu {
    margin:0;
    padding:0;
}
</STYLE>
